        "findings": "VERDICT: CHANGES REQUESTED\n\nThe endpoint integration is idiomatic: `CheckOwnerCityCapacity` mirrors the existing `Check<X>` steps (`CheckOwnerTelephoneUnique`, `CheckOwnerHouseholdUnique`) \u2014 a small single-method function, placed before `build`, governed by `transaction`, throwing a checked exception with a matching handler and escalation YAML. The exception/handler/YAML triad is exactly the project convention and the Javadoc cross-references are a nice touch. That part is clean.\n\nOne structural finding:\n\n- **Duplicated per-city count logic.** `CheckOwnerCityCapacity.service` computes the number of owners in a city with the exact same expression already present in `AssignOwnerCustomerCode.service`:\n  ```java\n  ownerRepository.findAll().stream()\n      .filter(existing -> city.equals(existing.getCity()))\n      .count()\n  ```\n  Both classes now encode the same domain primitive \u2014 \"how many owners are in this city\" \u2014 and the new Javadoc even ties itself to `AssignOwnerCustomerCode`'s per-city sequence, so the two are meant to stay consistent but can silently drift (e.g. if city matching is ever made case-insensitive to align with the household normalization). This codebase already shows the right pattern for shared owner domain logic: `CheckOwnerHouseholdUnique` delegates to the `OwnerHousehold` helper rather than inlining comparison logic. Follow that precedent \u2014 extract a single `countOwnersInCity(OwnerRepository, String city)` helper (an `OwnerCity`-style helper class, or a repository method) and have both `CheckOwnerCityCapacity` and `AssignOwnerCustomerCode` call it, so the \"owners in a city\" definition lives in one place.\n\nNothing else \u2014 the capacity constant, naming, and wiring are all natural for a maintainer here."
      }
